STDIO(3)                       P32W Unix                        STDIO(3)

NAME
       stdio, stdin, stdout, stderr - standard buffered input and output

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       FILE *stdin, *stdout, *stderr;

DESCRIPTION
       The functions of stdio give buffered input and output on top
       of the system calls read(2) and write(2). A stream is a
       pointer to a FILE; it is made by fopen(3) or fdopen(3), and
       three are open when a program starts:

       stdin     descriptor 0, read
       stdout    descriptor 1, written
       stderr    descriptor 2, written, unbuffered

       Buffers are BUFSIZ (512) bytes. stdout is line-buffered (each
       newline writes the line) when it is a terminal, and fully
       buffered (written when 512 bytes have gathered) when it is a
       file or a pipe; the choice is made at its first output, by
       isatty(3). stderr is unbuffered: each character is a write(2)
       of its own. Streams opened with fopen or fdopen are fully
       buffered, in 512 bytes from malloc(3). Reading stdin when its
       buffer is empty first flushes stdout, so a prompt without a
       newline appears before the program waits for the answer.

       exit(3), and so a return from main, flushes every stream
       open for writing; _exit (exit(2)) and death by a signal do not.

       At most FOPEN_MAX (16) streams are open at once, the three
       standard ones included. EOF (-1) is the value the character
       functions return at the end of the input or on an error.

       The functions, by page:

       fopen(3)     fopen, fdopen, freopen, fclose, fflush,
                    remove
       getc(3)      getc, fgetc, getchar, ungetc, fgets
       putc(3)      putc, fputc, putchar, fputs, puts
       fread(3)     fread, fwrite
       fseek(3)     fseek, ftell, rewind
       ferror(3)    ferror, feof, clearerr, fileno
       printf(3)    printf, fprintf, sprintf, snprintf
       vprintf(3)   vprintf, vfprintf, vsprintf, vsnprintf
       scanf(3)     scanf, fscanf, sscanf, vscanf, vfscanf,
                    vsscanf
       perror(3)    perror
       setvbuf(3)   setvbuf, setbuf
       tmpfile(3)   tmpfile, tmpnam, mkstemp
       popen(3)     popen, pclose

       stdio.h also defines NULL and size_t (from stddef.h), va_list
       (it includes stdarg.h), and SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR and SEEK_END
       (0, 1, 2) for fseek.

SEE ALSO
       open(2), read(2), write(2), close(2), intro(3), fopen(3),
       printf(3)

BUGS
       There is no gets, freopen, setbuf, setvbuf, tmpfile, tmpnam,
       popen or getline.

       getc, putc, getchar and putchar are functions, not macros, so
       each character costs a call.

       A stream opened for both reading and writing ("r+", "w+",
       "a+") has one buffer, used one way at a time; see fopen(3).
